{"id": "L14", "command": "submit", "inputs": {"recommendation": {"decision": "keep", "challenger": null, "diagnosis": "The champion's error eased compared with the prior half-year, but it still trails a ridge model built on markup and lagged-residual features, which beat it in every corridor during development and on the hidden window. The challenger failed promotion only because its risk band was too wide: it breached on too few days, so its uncertainty is miscalibrated and the rule does not allow a switch.", "evidence": [{"claim": "The champion's error over the last 26 weeks was 56.51 bps vs 68.69 bps before (-17.73%).", "ledger_ids": ["L02"]}, {"claim": "In development, the tuned ridge on markup and lagged residuals had lower error than the champion: 53.13 bps vs 56.51 bps (-5.98%), and lower in every corridor (MX 69.8 bps vs 74.49 bps, IN 14.66 bps vs 15.47 bps, BR 75.04 bps vs 79.7 bps).", "ledger_ids": ["L12"]}, {"claim": "Adding calendar features to the ridge did not help: 54.48 bps with calendar vs 53.53 bps without.", "ledger_ids": ["L05", "L07"]}, {"claim": "In the promotion test the challenger had lower error, 46.32 bps vs 49.96 bps (-7.29%), with a Diebold-Mariano p-value of 0.045, but its risk band breach rate of 0.012 was below the required band, so the promotion failed.", "ledger_ids": ["L13"]}], "risks": "Keeping the champion leaves a consistent accuracy gain unused, and the champion may keep losing ground to the feature-based ridge, especially in the MX and BR corridors where its error is largest and the MX markup is rising. The challenger's advantage could also be partly noise in a short hidden window.", "watch_next_week": "Re-test the ridge on markup and lagged residuals with attention to its risk band calibration, since an overly wide band was the only reason it failed; watch the MX markup trend and whether the champion's early-week errors keep exceeding its weekend errors."}}, "result": {"accepted": true, "errors": [], "attempt": 1}, "time": "2026-11-15T13:33:54Z"}
